# NEGATIVE CONTROL for `zone_invariant coherent`. NOT a test of the module.
#
# HOW IT IS RIGGED
#   `coherent` asserts that every answering worker read the SAME value. Pointed
#   at a genuinely shared field it holds, which is what case 4 of
#   shm-coherence-varidx.rule asserts about zone.ct_digest.
#
#   This fixture points it at `pid` instead -- the answering worker's process
#   id, which is rendered by the generic probe and is the ONE field in the
#   document that is per-worker by definition. The scenario's whole premise is
#   that a fanout of this width reaches several distinct workers (the coverage
#   oracle FAILS the case otherwise, so a run that somehow reached one worker
#   reddens here too, just with a different message). Several distinct workers
#   mean several distinct pids, so `coherent pid` is unsatisfiable exactly when
#   the fanout did its job.
#
#   That is a stronger control than folding a fake divergence into the module
#   would be: it needs no code change, it reddens against the SHIPPING binary,
#   and it fails for the one reason `coherent` exists to detect -- two workers
#   disagreeing about a value the rule claimed they should share.
#
# EXPECTED FAILURE MESSAGE (the driver greps for its stable prefix):
#   zone_invariant coherent pid: worker <pid> read <x>, worker <pid> read <y>
#
# NOTE ON THE FIELD NAME IN THE GREP: the driver matches on
# `zone_invariant coherent pid:` because that is the assertion this
# fixture writes below. Keep the two in step -- a fixture asserting one field
# while the driver greps for another would report "the control ran and proved
# nothing", which is the correct answer to that mistake and not a bug.
name    NEGATIVE CONTROL: coherent must reject a field the workers genuinely disagree about
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET /varidx/a HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nAccept-Encoding: gzip\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
expect  body~ct-varidx-payload
pid_may_change
probe   pid >= 1
fanout  24 2
zone_invariant coherent pid
